        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "11cbae1f5a2a71c736c80d504d379d9f6945a680",
            "parents": [
                "37490639ab033f13d456babeb3de9df30d6c532d"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/33/86033/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Marc Olberding",
                "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                "username": "molbear"
            },
            "createdOn": 1766018558,
            "author": {
                "name": "Marc Olberding",
                "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                "username": "molbear"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would the IANA be a meson option and not something we get from entity-manager?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "The thought was that BMC update today is a singleton already (i.e. bmcweb hard codes the association on lookup, iirc). I had a patch for getting it from entity-manager, but moved to this because the runtime path is simpler, and we didn't seem to sacrifice much for it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "Am I missing usecases where we can't set it through meson options (i.e. we can't know the identifiers at build) or do you have a preference for entity-manager?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Am I missing usecases where we can't set it through meson options (i.e. we can't know the identifiers at build) or do you have a preference for entity-manager?\n\nI guess I don't have a lot of skin in this decision.\n\nI don't know if you have any cases where you want to restrict the image to be updated based on the EEPROM detection of the hardware.  IBM does some stuff where they have a minimal device tree and then they do some detection and load a more robust device tree.  Maybe that's still all one image.\n\nIf you have image A1 -> An, then using the image settings (via Meson config) is fine.  If you have image A1 -> Bn, where they're not identical in IANA, you'd need EEPROM data to be able to do that.\n\nGenerally, I like having it in the entity-manager data just because that's where we have all the other IANA information and I don't like the proliferation of meson options, but it isn't a hard requirement."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "That's fair, I can definitely understand wanting uniformity. If we want to go with entity-manager just to avoid exceptions in how we define this, I'm fine with that. \n\nFor the An -> Bn, its not clear to me what situation you'd run into where this wouldn't work, its very possible I'm just being dense. If you changed device identifiers across a firmware boundary, you're going to run into a situation where you'd need to build a new fwpkg with both sets of descriptors to transition cleanly across that boundary, no? or just build a new version of An with the new device identifiers. In any case, you won't have to deal with incompatibility at update time, just after it boots, at which point you'd have to have a new package anyways to match the new identifiers.\n\ni.e. if you're on image A, with device identifiers x and y, you can still build a fwpkg where the image being updated to will report z and c. But after update you can't update back to image A without also packaging device descriptors support for x and y, or rebuilding the binary into a \"transitory package\". Its not clear to me how having this in entity-manager mitigates this problem. Are you saying that people may want to be able to advertise what device descriptors they support based off of which eeprom they find, i.e. one image for any number of actual configurations, but they are advertising it under different device descriptors based on eeprom (maybe off of product_sku, for example)?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is this ROTless or rootless?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "ROTless. this is really just \"self update,\" i.e. update via cp to /run/initramfs and reboot."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 224,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "We generally want all options `enabled` in CI.  Having it be disabled means we have code that isn't even compile-tested in CI.\n\nAre we comfortable that the code between the two of them is minimal enough that it is acceptable to have only one path compiled?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 224,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "Most of the code is under an ifconstexpr, so it will at least have syntactic checking. That said, I'm fine with having it enabled in CI. I'm open to feedback on how to deal with this in general, but I didn't love having compile time code switches, this just seemed to be the least invasive change and the least risky way to introduce this change. That said, it will have a PACKAGECONFIG switch in yocto anyways, so not sure there's much actual risk to have this default to enabled."
                }
            ],
            "files": [
                {
